[{'title': 'A career dedicated to innovation: Scott Inwood', 'paragraphs': "A message from the Office of the Vice-President, Research and International.\nScott\xa0Inwood's career has been driven by a natural curiosity for scientific discovery and a passion for transforming innovative ideas into real-world impact.\xa0\xa0Before joining the University, he built a diverse professional foundation through technical and business development roles in the chemical and water treatment sectors, developed and delivered training programs connecting the Ontario government and college network, and co-founded a clean technology start-up company. These experiences gave him a firsthand appreciation for the journey from idea to\xa0impact\xa0and set the stage for the work that would define his career.\nIt was\xa0that\xa0combination of scientific curiosity and hard-won commercial instinct that made Scott a natural fit for technology transfer.\xa0\xa0Scott joined the University of Waterloo in 1997 and assumed the role of Director,\xa0Commercialization of the Waterloo Commercialization Office (WatCo) in 2007.\xa0\xa0\xa0Over his nearly\xa03 decade\xa0career,\xa0he has managed all facets of the University’s investment in research-derived intellectual property assets, negotiated hundreds of commercial deals, supported the launch of start-up companies, and built a network of private sector relationships all channeled to translate\xa0world-class\xa0Waterloo research discoveries towards\xa0economic and societal impact both in Canada and around the world.\nScott has earned international recognition as a thought leader in technology management.\xa0His\xa0expertise\xa0has been\xa0sought\xa0across North America, Europe, Asia, and\xa0Oceania, including\xa0the Conference Board of Canada, the Association of University Technology Managers (AUTM), the OECD, and the US State Department - Hollings Centre. He has also served as an expert witness before the Canadian Parliamentary Standing Committee on Industry, Science and Technology and has supported the next generation of innovation leaders through teaching and mentorship in collaboration with the Intellectual Property Institute of Canada (IPIC).\nWhile Scott is retiring from his role at the University, his passion for supporting innovators will continue. In retirement, he plans to pursue consulting and voluntary roles to continue sharing his experience\xa0with\xa0aspiring innovators and knowledge transfer organizations.\nPlease join us in celebrating Scott and thanking him for\xa0nearly three\xa0decades of exceptional leadership, service, and lasting contributions to the University of Waterloo's innovation ecosystem. We wish him all the best in his new journey.\n", 'images': [{'alt': 'Scott Inwood.', 'src': '/daily-bulletin/sites/default/files/uploads/images/0724inwood.jpg'}], 'links': [{'text': '', 'href': None}]}, {'title': 'Waterloo computer scientists receive more than $1.3 million in federal funding', 'paragraphs': "This article wasoriginally published on the Cheriton School of Computer Science's website.\nTwenty-one\xa0researchers\xa0at\xa0the\xa0Cheriton School of Computer Science\xa0have\xa0secured\xa0a combined\xa0$1,331,500\xa0in\xa0funding\xa0through the\xa0Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada.\nThis year,NSERC is investing $630 millionto\xa0foster\xa0discovery\xa0research,\xa0training\xa0and talent\xa0development\xa0in Canada.\xa0The\xa0funding will\xa0empower\xa0researchers\xa0to\xa0tackle pressing problems\xa0and\xa0strengthen\xa0Canada’s position\xa0as a\xa0global innovation\xa0hub.\nMore than100 researchers at the University of Waterlooare part of this\xa0funding announcement.\n“Canada’s greatest competitive advantage is our people,” says\xa0the Honourable Mélanie Joly, Minister of Industry\xa0and Minister responsible for Canada Economic Development for Quebec Regions. “By investing in our world-class researchers and innovators right here at home, we are helping turn Canadian discoveries into\xa0new technologies, growing companies, and good jobs. At a time of rapid global change we are backing Canadian talent, strengthening our economy, and ensuring Canada\xa0remains\xa0a global leader in innovation, competitiveness, and resilience.”\nAt the\xa0centre\xa0of this investment is\xa0NSERC’sDiscovery Grants\xa0(DG)\xa0program, one of\xa0Canada’s\xa0most competitive\xa0and\xa0prestigious\xa0research\xa0funding\xa0programs.\xa0Unlike project-specific funding, Discovery Grants give researchers the flexibility to follow promising discoveries wherever they lead, creating space for breakthrough ideas that may not fit within traditional research boundaries.\nResearchers in the\xa0early stages\xa0of their academic careers can also receive\xa0additional\xa0support through theDiscovery Launch Supplements\xa0(DLS)\xa0program,\xa0helping them build research teams, attract\xa0students\xa0and\xa0establish\xa0momentum in their fields.\xa0TheResearch Tools and Instruments\xa0(RTI)\xa0grants\xa0programensures\xa0researchers have access to the advanced tools, equipment and lab infrastructure needed to strengthen research capacity, accelerate\xa0discovery\xa0and transform ideas into solutions.\nResearchers at\xa0the Cheriton\xa0School\xa0of Computer Science\xa0are\xa0advancing\xa0a wide range of\xa0projects\xa0spanning medicine, sustainable\xa0computing, software systems,\xa0data\xa0science\xa0and artificial intelligence, including efforts to improve\xa0the\xa0accountability\xa0and trustworthiness of\xa0AI\xa0systems.\nRead the full story\n", 'images': [], 'links': [{'text': '', 'href': None}, {'text': "originally published on the Cheriton School of Computer Science's website", 'href': 'https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/news/waterloo-computer-scientists-receive-more-than-13m-federal'}, {'text': 'NSERC is investing $630 million', 'href': 'https://nserc-crsng.canada.ca/en/news/minister-joly-announces-over-630-million-federal-investment-canadian-research-strengthen'}, {'text': '100 researchers at the University of Waterloo', 'href': 'https://uwaterloo.ca/news/waterloo-researchers-awarded-more-71-million-funding'}, {'text': 'Discovery Grants\xa0(DG)\xa0program', 'href': 'https://nserc-crsng.canada.ca/en/funding-opportunity/discovery-grants-program-individual'}, {'text': 'Discovery Launch Supplements\xa0(DLS)\xa0program', 'href': 'https://nserc-crsng.canada.ca/en/funding-opportunity/discovery-launch-supplements'}, {'text': 'Research Tools and Instruments\xa0(RTI)\xa0grants\xa0program', 'href': 'https://nserc-crsng.canada.ca/en/funding-opportunity/research-tools-and-instruments-grants-program'}, {'text': 'Read the full story', 'href': 'https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/news/waterloo-computer-scientists-receive-more-than-13m-federal'}]}, {'title': 'Q & A with the Experts: Drowning prevention', 'paragraphs': 'Each year, approximately 50 people drown in the Great Lakes due to waves and currents. National Drowning Prevention Week and World Drowning Prevention Day raise awareness about safety risks in and around water, and share actions that people can take to stay safe (Getty Images/Benedek).\nThis article wasoriginally published on Waterloo News.\nDr. Chris Houser, Dean of the Faculty of Science at the University of Waterloo,\xa0studies Canada’s coastlines and\xa0contributes to\xa0efforts to reduce\xa0the incidence of drowning and\xa0emergency rescues.\xa0He shares safety tips\xa0as part of\xa0National Drowning Prevention Week.\nWhat are the leading causes of drowning in the Great Lakes?\nImportant contributing factors\xa0in the Great Lakes\xa0include breaking waves and dangerous currents, sudden drop-offs,\xa0cold water, fatigue, limited swimming ability, alcohol use, inadequate\xa0supervision\xa0and failure to wear a lifejacket while boating or paddling. Piers,\xa0breakwalls, river mouths and other structures can create especially dangerous currents.\nWhat are\xa0the dangerous conditions\xa0in the\xa0Great Lakes?\nThe\xa0waves are closely spaced and can repeatedly strike a swimmer, providing little time to recover\xa0and\xa0exhausting\xa0even a capable swimmer. Rip currents form when waves push water toward the shore and that accumulated water flows back toward deeper water through a narrow channel.\nHow do rip currents cause drowning?\nA rip current carries the swimmer away from shore. The danger begins when a person panics and tries to swim directly against the current. Even strong swimmers can become exhausted.\xa0The current can also move someone suddenly into deeper water.\xa0People sometimes choose\xa0the calm-looking gap between breaking waves\xa0because it appears safer,\xa0but\xa0it may be where water is flowing most strongly away from shore.\nWhat do you do if you are caught in a rip current?\nThe simple message is flip, float and follow.\xa0Flip or lean back and float. Follow the current temporarily rather than fighting it. Most rip currents weaken beyond the breaking waves or as they spread out.\xa0Swim across the current,\xa0generally parallel\xa0to the shoreline, when you can do so.\xa0If you cannot escape, continue floating or treading water and wave an arm or call for help.\nWhat else\xa0can people do to prevent\xa0drowning?\nCheck the beach and marine forecast before leaving home and again\xa0on site.\xa0Swim at a lifeguarded beach and within the designated swimming area.\xa0Stay out of the water when waves are high or a red flag is flying.\xa0Avoid swimming beside piers,\xa0breakwalls\xa0and river outlets.\xa0Never swim alone.\xa0Keep children within arm’s reach and\xa0designate\xa0an undistracted adult watcher.\xa0Wear a properly fitted lifejacket while boating,\xa0paddling\xa0or fishing, and whenever swimming ability or conditions are uncertain.\xa0\xa0Avoid alcohol and cannabis before or during aquatic activities.\xa0Learn CPR and basic rescue techniques that do not require entering the water.\n', 'images': [{'alt': 'Waves of Lake Ontario lap onto the shore.', 'src': '/daily-bulletin/sites/default/files/uploads/images/0724drowningprevention.jpeg'}], 'links': [{'text': '', 'href': None}, {'text': 'originally published on Waterloo News', 'href': 'https://uwaterloo.ca/news/q-experts-drowning-prevention'}]}]